/*!
    \page ascent-setup.html
    \title Project Setup and the Game Loop
    \previouspage Ascent
    \nextpage Provably Fair Randomness

    This chapter gets a window on screen and the multiplier curve behind it. By
    the end you will have an empty but running Felgo game, a C++ class that turns
    a duration into a multiplier, and that class talking to QML.

    \section1 Creating the Project

    Create a new Felgo project from Qt Creator's wizard, or start from an empty
    directory with a \c CMakeLists.txt. The Felgo-specific part of it is short:

    \snippet CMakeLists.txt felgo-project

    \c find_package(Felgo) brings in everything Felgo ships - the QML types, the
    plugins, the deployment helpers. The product variables underneath it are what
    end up in the app bundle: the identifier that identifies your app to the app
    stores, the version the store shows, and the license key. Leave the key empty
    while you build. It only removes the Felgo splash screen, and the splash
    screen turns out to be something we have to handle anyway.

    \c PRODUCT_STAGE stays at \c test until the very end, when switching it to
    \c publish is part of preparing a release build.

    \section1 The Game Window and the Scenes

    A Felgo game starts with a \c GameWindow holding one or more scenes:

    \snippet qml/AscentMain.qml game-window

    \c GameWindow is the application window, and \c screenWidth and
    \c screenHeight only decide how big it opens on a desktop - on a phone it
    fills the screen instead.

    \c Scene is where resolution independence happens. You give it a logical size
    once and then position everything inside it in those coordinates forever;
    Felgo scales the scene to whatever display it lands on and letterboxes the
    remainder. Here that size is given once, in a \c SceneBase every scene
    inherits, rather than repeated per scene:

    \code
    Scene {
      id: root

      width: 320
      height: 600

      opacity: 0
      visible: opacity > 0
      enabled: visible
    }
    \endcode

    Portrait, because the balloon rises - and it is the right shape for a phone.
    Taller than the window's own ratio on purpose: the extra logical height is
    room the layout can hand to the balloon, and it costs nothing, because the
    scene is scaled rather than cropped.

    The last two lines of that base are the ones worth copying. A hidden scene is
    invisible, and in Qt an invisible item is still \e enabled - its buttons keep
    taking taps. Tying \c enabled to \c visible is what stops the menu being
    pressable through the game.

    The sky is anchored to \c fullWindowAnchorItem rather than to the scene. The
    scene is the play area, but the window may be taller or wider than it, and
    without that anchor the letterbox bars would show through as black.

    \section1 Switching Scenes

    Which scene is on screen is one property on the window:

    \snippet qml/AscentMain.qml scene-states

    A state names a scene and makes it visible; the state machine reverses the
    change on the way out, so nothing has to remember to hide the scene it left.
    \c activeScene moves with it, which is what gives the visible scene the key
    focus.

    Scenes do not name their successor. Each one emits \c backRequested and the
    window decides where that goes, so a scene can be opened from anywhere
    without knowing what it will be returning to.

    The game opens on the game rather than on the menu. Rounds cycle whether
    anyone is watching, so a title screen between the player and the next round
    would be a door with nothing behind it.

    \section1 From Elapsed Time to a Multiplier

    Now the first piece of actual game logic. A crash game's multiplier grows
    exponentially: slowly at the start, then faster and faster. That shape is
    what makes waiting stop feeling free at some point, which is the entire
    tension of the game.

    \snippet src/CrashCurve.cpp multiplier-at

    Two things are worth pointing out. The first is what the function does
    \e not do: it holds no state about the round. It does not know whether a
    round is running, when it started, or whether anyone has bet. You give it a
    duration and it gives you a multiplier. That makes it trivial to unit test -
    no clock to fake, no round to set up - and safe to call from a paint loop.

    The second is the guard on negative time. Clocks hand you odd deltas around a
    round boundary, and a negative one should not mean negative winnings. A round
    pays from 1.00x upwards, so anything before the start simply \e is the start.

    The growth rate is a property rather than a constant, so it can be tuned from
    QML while the game runs. At the default of 0.1 per second the multiplier
    doubles roughly every seven seconds, which is a pace players can feel.

    \section1 Letting QML See the Core

    The curve is a \c QObject with a \c Q_PROPERTY and a \c Q_INVOKABLE method,
    which is all QML needs. It just has to be told the type exists:

    \snippet main.cpp register-core-types

    Notice that QML is not handed the means to build any of this. A running game
    has exactly one wallet and one stream of rounds, so those two objects are
    registered as singletons: QML can read \c {PlayerWallet.balance} and call
    \c {Rounds.requestCashOut()}, but it cannot invent a second wallet, and a
    second wallet is precisely the kind of bug that only shows up once someone
    has real points in it.

    The round engine is registered too, as an uncreatable type. That sounds
    pointless until you need \c {RoundEngine.Running} in a binding - registering
    it is what makes the state names exist in QML, without offering to
    instantiate an engine that would have nothing to run.

    Build and run, and the window comes up dark and empty. It is not a game yet -
    nothing moves and there is nothing to press - but the two halves of the app
    are connected, and every chapter from here on adds to a running program.

    \include includes/chapters.qdocinc chapters
*/
